{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-04T07:07:25.654Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"94","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed on the PR branch (a17823f2). CONFIRMED: the digest cache added to avoid rehashing an ~80MB binary four times keyed on PATH alone, so a rebuild between capture sites went unrecorded — capture 1 and capture 2 both reported sha256:372a7226cea0... while the truth was 3d7fbf697646.... This is precisely the failure the implementation inventory exists to prevent, reintroduced by the optimisation that made the inventory affordable: the artifact would bind the build the run STARTED with while the adapters ran different code for the rest of it.\n\nFix keys on (path, st_dev, st_ino, st_size, st_mtime_ns) — one stat per capture instead of a full rehash, and any change recomputes. The finding's other option, recomputing at every capture, was declined on cost: unconditional rehashing is four full passes over ~80MB per arm per run, and stat-based invalidation is how build systems solve exactly this. The residual is stated in the docstring rather than left implicit — a replacement preserving device, inode, size AND nanosecond mtime would still be missed.\n\nBoth directions verified. Fail-without-fix: 'the artifact claimed the original build'. A companion test (test_an_unchanged_binary_is_hashed_once) pins the cache itself, so a later editor cannot 'fix' the staleness by deleting the cache and silently paying 4x the hashing.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NKD3GRWN5FH5QFPJ4PYFB5","targetId":"01M1NKD3GZ28HKG115Q0ZAYYWJ","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NC2KDB8G41TMVXFNRMSDX4"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
