{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-04T06:01:44.468Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"24","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed on the PR branch (e9d20fe8). CONFIRMED, and note the direction: this is NOT a leak but its inverse — provenance loss. The redactor tested for a leading '-' before considering whether a token was the pending value of an allowlisted option, so negative numbers were mistaken for unknown flags and dropped: ['run.py','--seed','\u003credacted\u003e','--max-questions','\u003credacted\u003e','--top-k','200']. Worth fixing precisely because the point of this block is that the artifact says what actually ran.\n\nThe finding's BROADER phrasing was deliberately NOT taken. It proposed consuming pending_safe_value before interpreting a leading '-' as an option; that reintroduces the leak this file has already been fixed for three times — '--top-k --mem0-api-key=sk-SECRET' would record the second token verbatim as --top-k's value. Implemented narrowly instead: a pending value is consumed only when the token matches argparse's own _negative_number_matcher (-\\d+$|-\\d*\\.\\d+$). argparse rejects that malformed command line anyway ('expected one argument'), so the narrow rule loses nothing real while keeping the guarantee, and the guard has its own test (test_a_negative_number_is_not_a_licence_to_consume_any_token). Verified '--seed -1 --max-questions -1 --rpm -2.5' all survive intact and '--debug -1' (a toggle with no pending value) still redacts conservatively.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NFRGNAA9V2A5YY9RESEQC7","targetId":"01M1NFRGNF7169JCJ0BAGA36VX","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NC2KDB8G41TMVXFNRMSDX4"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
