{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:21:59.839Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"146","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"CONFIRMED -- a real regression I introduced in a7261b8c, reproduced as a permanent hang, fixed in aaa2d131.\n\nReproduced first. A temp repo with one untracked symlink to /dev/zero:\n  git ls-files --others --exclude-standard  -\u003e  'zero_link'\n  os.stat('zero_link').st_size = 0   (S_ISCHR: True)\n  git_state(root) did not return within 10s\nos.stat follows the link, the device reports size 0, so it passes any byte budget, and _file_digest then loops on 'fh.read(1 \u003c\u003c 20)' which never returns b''. Metadata capture reads forever. The premise is exactly right and a byte budget is no defence against it.\n\nFix (bench/memory/benchmarks/common/runmeta.py, _untracked_digest_lines):\n  entry  = os.lstat(path)                       # never follows\n  linked = stat.S_ISLNK(entry.st_mode)\n  target = os.stat(path) if linked else entry\n  if not stat.S_ISREG(target.st_mode):\n      lines.append(f\"{name}\\0{label}not-a-regular-file mode:{target.st_mode:#o}\")\n      continue\nNothing that is not a regular file is ever opened. Devices, FIFOs, sockets and directories are recorded by mode; a dangling link or symlink loop raises OSError from lstat/stat and is recorded 'unreadable'.\n\nOne deviation from the prescribed remedy, deliberate: it said to reject symlinks outright. Symlinks are followed, but only to a regular file. An untracked implementation file may legitimately be a symlink and its target is what actually ran, so rejecting the class would lose exactly the provenance this digest exists to bind. The entry is labelled 'symlink ' so replacing a file with a link to identical bytes still moves the digest -- the substitution that a blanket reject would have made invisible. Documented residual, in place: a regular file swapped for a device between the stat and the open would still be read; closing that needs O_NONBLOCK plus an fstat, which no non-adversarial checkout needs.\n\nPROOF (compile-clean revert). Restoring only the previous os.stat-without-a-mode-check loop body, keeping 'import stat' so nothing breaks at import (ast.parse: 'compiles clean'), then\n  python3 -B -m unittest bench.memory.benchmarks.common.test_runmeta\nfails 2 of 67, and the suite's wall clock goes from 1.4s to 16.6s:\n\nFAIL: test_an_untracked_device_symlink_is_never_read\n  self.assertLess(elapsed, 5.0, ...)\n  AssertionError: 15.001509250025265 not less than 5.0 : git_state spent 15.0s reading a character device\n  (15s is the test's SIGALRM watchdog firing inside the read; without a watchdog the call never returns at all. _file_digest catches OSError and TimeoutError is one, so on the reverted body the entry comes back as 'zero_link\\x00unreadable: TimeoutError' -- the read happened.)\n\nFAIL: test_a_symlinked_untracked_file_is_followed_and_labelled\n  AssertionError: 'sha256:72f20db519da' == 'sha256:72f20db519da' : a file swapped for a link to identical bytes\n\nWith the fix in place: 'Ran 67 tests ... OK', and the full CI guard set (test_runmeta + ci.test_locomo_search_retry_patch) 'Ran 78 tests ... OK'.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1S5JC05W18V7E4HTPHV58N8","targetId":"01M1S5JC1GRC1571K88JMN6SN5","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NC2KDB8G41TMVXFNRMSDX4"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
{"data":{"actorId":null,"createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:22:01.163Z","eventType":"monitor.updated","id":"147","payload":{"boolean_value":null,"head_sha":"aaa2d1312658aa9c635d87139cd864845c85bf55","label":"Risk","monitor_key":"risk","percent_value":22,"polarity":"lower_is_better","rationale":"All changes are confined to the bench/memory Python benchmark harness (run-metadata redaction, two arm patches, tests) plus a CI workflow that runs its guards; nothing touches the graph schema/wire contract, symbol IDs, parser, streaming, public CLI, no-egress core, or CGO builds. Risk is bounded to benchmark provenance/fairness with a secret-redaction trust boundary, but that surface is hardened and covered by an extensive new test suite and CI guard.","run_id":"01M1S5Z4V8HXH942PJCDC2PVJM","runner_id":"trail-risk","size_value":null,"value_type":"percent"},"reviewId":null,"targetId":"risk","targetType":"trail_monitor","trailId":"01M1NC2KDB8G41TMVXFNRMSDX4"},"event":"monitor.updated"}
{"data":{"actorId":null,"createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:22:06.385Z","eventType":"monitor.updated","id":"148","payload":{"boolean_value":null,"head_sha":"aaa2d1312658aa9c635d87139cd864845c85bf55","label":"Security","monitor_key":"security","percent_value":8,"polarity":"lower_is_better","rationale":"All changes are defensive hardening of a benchmark provenance artifact: allowlist-based argv/env secret redaction (fixing a previously inverted filter that leaked credential-carrying vars), bounded in-repo untracked-file digesting with safe symlink/device handling, and a locked-down CI job (contents:read, persist-credentials:false, pinned actions, stdlib-only). Every subprocess call is argv-vector with no shell/eval, and there is no new network, dependency, or runtime-download path.","run_id":"01M1S5Z4VMA4KPFTN6FHN61S7Q","runner_id":"trail-security","size_value":null,"value_type":"percent"},"reviewId":null,"targetId":"security","targetType":"trail_monitor","trailId":"01M1NC2KDB8G41TMVXFNRMSDX4"},"event":"monitor.updated"}
{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-05T16:24:00.445Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"149","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"PREMISE CONFIRMED, remedy UNDERDETERMINED -- filed as https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/issues/217 rather than guessed at.\n\nReproduced. git_state() on a plain (non-git) directory:\n  A: {'commit': '', 'dirty': False}\n  B: {'commit': '', 'dirty': False}      # after rewriting impl.py in place\n  IDENTICAL: True\n  provenance entry: {'path': 'sha256:ed862663f2b2', 'source': 'env', 'commit': '', 'dirty': False}\nSo yes: two different source trees at one path produce byte-identical provenance for GRAPHIFY_SOURCE. Not disputed.\n\nBoth prescribed remedies were tested, and both break something:\n\n1. 'fail closed' aborts the kit's actual deployment shape. The bench kit is copied to VMs as a plain tree, not cloned. Copying bench/memory outside any checkout and calling capture() succeeds today -- 'git rev-parse --show-toplevel' -\u003e 'fatal: not a git repository', git block {'commit': '', 'dirty': False}, and 8 code_md5 entries still binding the harness. Failing closed turns every VM run into an abort, which is a policy change to when the harness refuses to run.\n\n2. 'hash the source tree' is unbounded. Without git there is no --exclude-standard, so nothing excludes __pycache__, .venv, build output or vendored data from an arbitrary checkout. This PR has already had to give the untracked digest an explicit 32 MiB / 512 file budget (a real tree carried a single 78 MB untracked artifact) and a regular-file guard (a symlink to /dev/zero hung capture forever, finding 01M1S5JC1GRC...). A source-tree hash inherits both and additionally needs an answer to 'which files are the implementation' -- an include list nobody has chosen.\n\nWorth recording, because it bounds the exposure: this gap is the ARM SOURCE CHECKOUT only. The harness itself is already content-bound without git -- code_hashes() md5s 8 files including runmeta.py and requirements-lock-py312.txt -- and does so precisely because the kit ships as a copied tree.\n\nDismissing rather than fixing is also a stop-condition call. runmeta.py provenance has now been flagged on three consecutive heads of this PR (dirty digest -\u003e device symlink -\u003e non-git source dir), each new finding raised against the previous fix. That is the thrash signal, and this remedy is a design decision, so it goes to #217 for the fairness-kit owner instead of a fourth speculative push.","to":"dismissed"},"reviewId":"01M1S60QTN4Q8YEJNJE0WNT3CF","targetId":"01M1S60QV9ER44KPYDRHR1XV5B","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NC2KDB8G41TMVXFNRMSDX4"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
